---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Security"
    categorySlug="guides/security"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Your Home Network Is Now Your Office Network</h2>
  <p>
    When you work from home, your personal WiFi network becomes your corporate network. The same
    connection your kids use for gaming and your smart TV uses for streaming is now handling
    sensitive work data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most home networks are set up for convenience, not security. Default passwords, outdated firmware,
    and unsecured IoT devices create vulnerabilities that attackers can exploit.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide walks you through securing your home network properly—protecting both your work and
    personal data from common threats.
  </p>

  <!-- Router Security Checklist -->
  <h2 id="checklist">Router Security Checklist</h2>
  <p>
    Here's a quick checklist of everything we'll cover. Use this to track your progress:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Change default router admin password</li>
    <li>Change default WiFi network name (SSID)</li>
    <li>Set a strong WiFi password (12+ characters)</li>
    <li>Enable WPA3 or WPA2-AES encryption</li>
    <li>Update router firmware to latest version</li>
    <li>Disable WPS (WiFi Protected Setup)</li>
    <li>Disable remote management</li>
    <li>Set up a guest network for IoT devices</li>
    <li>Enable firewall (usually on by default)</li>
    <li>Consider VPN on router for extra protection</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Let's go through each item in detail.
  </p>

  <!-- Change Default Passwords -->
  <h2 id="passwords">Change Default Passwords</h2>
  <p>
    Your router has two passwords that need attention:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Router Admin Password</h3>
  <p>
    This is the password to access your router's settings page. Default passwords like "admin/admin"
    are publicly known and must be changed.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Log into your router (usually 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.0.1)</li>
    <li>Find <strong>Administration</strong>, <strong>System</strong>, or <strong>Management</strong> settings</li>
    <li>Change the admin password to something unique and strong</li>
    <li>Store this password in your password manager</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2. WiFi Network Password</h3>
  <p>
    This is the password people use to connect to your WiFi. Requirements:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Length:</strong> At least 12 characters (16+ is better)</li>
    <li><strong>Complexity:</strong> Mix of letters, numbers, symbols</li>
    <li><strong>Uniqueness:</strong> Don't reuse passwords from other accounts</li>
    <li><strong>No personal info:</strong> Avoid names, addresses, birthdays</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A passphrase like <code>correct-horse-battery-staple-42!</code> is both secure and memorable.
  </p>

  <h3>Change Default Network Name (SSID)</h3>
  <p>
    Default names like "NETGEAR-5G" or "linksys" reveal your router brand, making targeted attacks
    easier. Change it to something that doesn't identify you or your equipment.
  </p>

  <!-- WiFi Encryption -->
  <h2 id="encryption">WiFi Encryption (WPA3 vs WPA2)</h2>
  <p>
    Encryption prevents anyone nearby from intercepting your WiFi traffic. Here's what to use:
  </p>

  <h3>WPA3 (Best)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Latest WiFi security standard (2018+)</li>
    <li>Stronger encryption, harder to crack</li>
    <li>Protection against offline dictionary attacks</li>
    <li>Forward secrecy—past traffic can't be decrypted if password is later compromised</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Use if:</strong> Your router and all devices support it.
  </p>

  <h3>WPA2-Personal (AES)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Previous standard, still secure with strong password</li>
    <li>Compatible with virtually all devices</li>
    <li>AES encryption is unbroken</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Use if:</strong> You have older devices that don't support WPA3.
  </p>

  <h3>WPA2/WPA3 Transitional Mode</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Uses WPA3 for capable devices, falls back to WPA2 for older ones</li>
    <li>Best compatibility while maximizing security</li>
    <li>Good default choice for most homes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Never Use</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>WEP:</strong> Broken, can be cracked in minutes</li>
    <li><strong>WPA (original):</strong> Also broken, vulnerable to attacks</li>
    <li><strong>Open networks:</strong> No encryption at all</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Guest Network -->
  <h2 id="guest-network">Creating a Guest Network</h2>
  <p>
    A guest network is a separate WiFi network that's isolated from your main network. It's essential
    for two purposes:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Actual Guests</h3>
  <p>
    When visitors ask for WiFi, give them guest network access. This way:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>They can't access your computers or network drives</li>
    <li>If their device is infected, it can't spread to your devices</li>
    <li>You don't have to share your main password</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. IoT and Smart Home Devices</h3>
  <p>
    Smart TVs, speakers, cameras, and other IoT devices often have poor security. Isolate them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Put all smart home devices on the guest network</li>
    <li>They can still reach the internet but can't access your work laptop</li>
    <li>If a device is compromised, damage is contained</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Setting Up Guest Network</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Access router admin panel</li>
    <li>Look for <strong>Guest Network</strong> or <strong>Guest WiFi</strong> settings</li>
    <li>Enable guest network</li>
    <li>Set a different password than your main network</li>
    <li>Enable <strong>AP Isolation</strong> or <strong>Client Isolation</strong> if available</li>
    <li>Consider bandwidth limits for guests</li>
  </ol>

  <!-- Firmware Updates -->
  <h2 id="firmware">Firmware Updates</h2>
  <p>
    Router firmware is the software running on your router. Like any software, it needs updates to
    fix security vulnerabilities.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Firmware Updates Matter</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Security patches:</strong> Fix known vulnerabilities attackers exploit</li>
    <li><strong>Bug fixes:</strong> Improve stability and performance</li>
    <li><strong>New features:</strong> Sometimes add WPA3, VPN support, etc.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to Update</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Log into router admin panel</li>
    <li>Find <strong>Firmware Update</strong>, <strong>Software Update</strong>, or <strong>Administration</strong></li>
    <li>Click <strong>Check for Updates</strong></li>
    <li>If available, click <strong>Update</strong> or <strong>Install</strong></li>
    <li>Wait for router to restart (don't unplug during update)</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Enable Automatic Updates</h3>
  <p>
    Many modern routers support automatic updates. Enable this if available:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ASUS:</strong> Administration → Firmware Upgrade → Auto Upgrade</li>
    <li><strong>Netgear:</strong> Advanced → Administration → Firmware Update</li>
    <li><strong>TP-Link:</strong> Advanced → System Tools → Firmware Upgrade</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If automatic updates aren't available, set a monthly calendar reminder to check manually.
  </p>

  <!-- Disable Risky Features -->
  <h2 id="disable-features">Disable Risky Features</h2>
  <p>
    Several common router features create security risks. Disable them:
  </p>

  <h3>WPS (WiFi Protected Setup)</h3>
  <p>
    WPS lets you connect devices by pressing a button or entering a PIN. The problem: the PIN can be
    brute-forced in hours.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Find WPS settings in your router admin panel</li>
    <li>Disable WPS completely</li>
    <li>Use regular password authentication instead</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Remote Management</h3>
  <p>
    Remote management lets you access your router from outside your home network. This is rarely needed
    and creates attack surface.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Disable "Remote Management" or "Remote Administration"</li>
    <li>Disable "Cloud Management" unless you specifically need it</li>
    <li>If you need remote access, use VPN to your home network instead</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>UPnP (Universal Plug and Play)</h3>
  <p>
    UPnP lets devices automatically open firewall ports. Convenient but risky:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Malware can use UPnP to bypass your firewall</li>
    <li>Consider disabling unless you need it for gaming or specific applications</li>
    <li>If you keep it enabled, monitor what ports are open</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Router VPN -->
  <h2 id="vpn">Setting Up VPN on Your Router</h2>
  <p>
    Installing a VPN directly on your router protects all devices on your network automatically—no
    need to install VPN apps on each device.
  </p>

  <h3>Benefits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>All devices protected, including those that don't support VPN apps</li>
    <li>One connection instead of multiple</li>
    <li>Smart TVs, gaming consoles, IoT devices all covered</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Considerations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Speed:</strong> All traffic goes through VPN, may reduce speeds</li>
    <li><strong>Compatibility:</strong> Not all routers support VPN client mode</li>
    <li><strong>Streaming:</strong> Some services block VPN connections</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How to Set Up</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Ensure your router supports VPN client mode (ASUS, Netgear, DD-WRT compatible routers)</li>
    <li>Get OpenVPN or WireGuard configuration from your VPN provider</li>
    <li>Access router admin panel → VPN → VPN Client</li>
    <li>Upload or enter configuration details</li>
    <li>Enable and test the connection</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>VPN Providers with good router support:</strong> NordVPN, ExpressVPN, Surfshark, Private Internet Access
  </p>

  <!-- Network Segmentation -->
  <h2 id="segmentation">Advanced: Network Segmentation</h2>
  <p>
    For serious security, consider segmenting your network into separate zones:
  </p>

  <h3>Why Segment?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Containment:</strong> If one device is compromised, others are protected</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> Work devices can't see personal devices and vice versa</li>
    <li><strong>Compliance:</strong> Some work policies require isolated networks</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Basic Segmentation with VLANs</h3>
  <p>
    VLANs (Virtual LANs) create separate networks on one physical network:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Work VLAN:</strong> Work laptop, work phone</li>
    <li><strong>Personal VLAN:</strong> Personal devices</li>
    <li><strong>IoT VLAN:</strong> Smart home devices</li>
    <li><strong>Guest VLAN:</strong> Visitors</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Router with VLAN support (many prosumer routers have this)</li>
    <li>Managed switch if you need wired VLAN separation</li>
    <li>Time to configure properly</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is advanced configuration. For most remote workers, a guest network for IoT devices
    provides 80% of the benefit with 20% of the complexity.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Secure Your Network Today</h2>
  <p>
    Securing your home network takes about 30 minutes and significantly reduces your risk. Start with
    the basics and add advanced features as needed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your action plan:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Today:</strong> Change default passwords, check encryption settings</li>
    <li><strong>This week:</strong> Update firmware, disable WPS, set up guest network</li>
    <li><strong>This month:</strong> Consider VPN on router, network segmentation for IoT</li>
    <li><strong>Ongoing:</strong> Check for firmware updates monthly</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your home network is the foundation of your remote work security. Take the time to secure it properly.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
